WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.749 --> 00:00:15.971
When Romeo and Juliet kissed goodbye in the early dawn after their wedding night, they didn't know where

2
00:00:16.172 --> 00:00:17.752
or when they might see each other next.

3
00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:22.455
Neither of them spoke of the worry that was deepest in their hearts out of fear that it

4
00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:25.537
might come true: they feared that they might never kiss again.

5
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:31.180
Romeo, with a penalty of death hanging over his head, escaped over the wall and set out for

6
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.071
Mantua. Juliet was still gazing from her balcony long after he had disappeared when her mother and father

7
00:00:37.111 --> 00:00:37.952
came into her room.

8
00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:40.774
"Are you still weeping for your cousin?"

9
00:00:41.354 --> 00:00:44.396
Lady Capulet asked when she saw the tears in her daughter's eyes.

10
00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:47.358
"You cannot wash him from his grave with tears."

11
00:00:47.378 --> 00:00:51.882
"I weep because the man who killed him has fled," said Juliet.

12
00:00:51.902 --> 00:00:55.524
"Yes, that murderer Romeo lives," said her father.

13
00:00:55.544 --> 00:00:59.607
"But take comfort, Juliet, we will have vengeance for Tybalt's death.

14
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:00.668
So weep no more."

15
00:01:02.748 --> 00:01:05.349
"Now we have happier news," began Lady Capulet.

16
00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:10.811
Together, she and Lord Capulet explained the arrangements they had made for Juliet to marry Paris in two

17
00:01:10.851 --> 00:01:12.892
days. "It's impossible!

18
00:01:13.232 --> 00:01:14.933
I can't," Juliet gasped.

19
00:01:15.793 --> 00:01:17.234
"Are you too proud to obey us?"

20
00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:18.535
her father raged.

21
00:01:19.155 --> 00:01:22.796
He had expected her to be grateful for their finding her such a worthy husband.

22
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:24.977
Juliet dropped to her knees.

23
00:01:24.997 --> 00:01:31.080
"Oh Father," she pleaded, "delay the marriage for a month, a week "I have given my word to

24
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.169
Count Paris; I will not take it back!

25
00:01:33.629 --> 00:01:35.231
On Thursday, you will marry him!"

26
00:01:36.111 --> 00:01:42.277
shouted Lord Capulet and, grabbing his wife's hand, he pulled her from the room, leaving Juliet still kneeling

27
00:01:42.337 --> 00:01:42.857
on the floor.

28
00:01:43.998 --> 00:01:48.182
At first, Juliet didn't know what to do, but she quickly composed herself.

29
00:01:49.122 --> 00:01:50.784
She put on her cloak and called her nurse.

30
00:01:50.804 --> 00:01:55.928
"Tell my mother I am sorry that I displeased Father and that I have gone to Friar Lawrence

31
00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:57.409
for confession," she said.

32
00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.993
When she arrived at the friar's cell, he called out to her from the door.

33
00:02:02.013 --> 00:02:05.615
"Ah, Juliet, Count Paris was just here.

34
00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:08.196
He asked me to perform your wedding on Thursday!

35
00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:09.357
This is terrible!"

36
00:02:10.457 --> 00:02:14.179
Juliet quickly slipped inside his cell and faced him with determination in her eyes.

37
00:02:14.199 --> 00:02:18.162
"God joined my heart to Romeo's when you joined our hands.

38
00:02:19.202 --> 00:02:23.204
I would rather lie dead beside Tybalt in the tomb than force my heart to be a traitor

39
00:02:23.244 --> 00:02:23.805
to Romeo."

40
00:02:24.945 --> 00:02:26.046
"Do you truly mean this?"

41
00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:29.748
the friar asked, because her words had suggested a desperate plan.

42
00:02:30.764 --> 00:02:35.347
The friar had a potion that would cause whoever drank it to appear cold and lifeless for 42

43
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:38.188
hours, but then awake as if from a deep slumber.

44
00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.611
He gave Juliet a small vial of the potion with instructions to drink it the night before her

45
00:02:43.671 --> 00:02:44.292
planned wedding.

46
00:02:45.212 --> 00:02:48.494
He explained that her parents, thinking her dead, would place her in the tomb.

47
00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:53.837
The friar promised to come secretly with Romeo to open the tomb when she awoke and take her

48
00:02:53.877 --> 00:02:54.457
to Mantua.

49
00:02:55.518 --> 00:02:59.240
Taking the vial in both hands, Juliet said, "Love will give me courage."

50
00:03:00.284 --> 00:03:04.466
As soon as she had gone, the friar hurriedly sat down to write a letter to Romeo.

51
00:03:05.546 --> 00:03:11.929
On Wednesday, Lord Capulet happily hired cooks and extra serving men, while Lady Capulet sent servants all over

52
00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:14.430
Verona to buy cakes and flowers for the wedding.

53
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:21.453
Both were in high spirits because, that morning, Juliet had apologized and quietly agreed to marry Count Paris.

54
00:03:22.653 --> 00:03:27.495
In the evening, Lady Capulet brought Juliet's wedding dress up to her room, along with flowers for her

55
00:03:27.555 --> 00:03:29.840
hair. "Farewell!"

56
00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.681
Juliet said when her mother had closed the door.

57
00:03:32.681 --> 00:03:38.102
She dressed herself in her wedding robe, wove the flowers into her hair, and retrieved the potion from

58
00:03:38.162 --> 00:03:38.843
under her pillow.

59
00:03:39.743 --> 00:03:41.123
She pulled the cap from the vial.

60
00:03:41.623 --> 00:03:42.704
For a moment she paused.

61
00:03:42.724 --> 00:03:45.864
"What if the friar is mistaken and I never wake?"

62
00:03:46.345 --> 00:03:46.785
she thought.

63
00:03:47.785 --> 00:03:50.786
But putting her fears aside, she raised the vial to her lips.

64
00:03:50.806 --> 00:03:55.007
"Romeo, I come," she said and drank.

65
00:03:55.947 --> 00:03:58.708
The next morning, Juliet's nurse brought breakfast to her room.

66
00:03:59.848 --> 00:04:01.929
"Why, little lamb, you've slept too long.

67
00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.211
Wake up," she called as she entered.

68
00:04:05.191 --> 00:04:09.514
When she saw Juliet lying on her bed as pale as death, she dropped the breakfast tray and

69
00:04:09.554 --> 00:04:12.475
screamed. The whole household came running.

70
00:04:13.396 --> 00:04:17.738
Her father was the last to reach the bedroom and pushed his way past Lady Capulet and the

71
00:04:17.778 --> 00:04:19.819
nurse, who were crying beside the bed.

72
00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.041
He shook his daughter, but she could not be roused.

73
00:04:24.202 --> 00:04:25.663
"Alas! My sweet Juliet!

74
00:04:25.903 --> 00:04:27.484
She's cold," he cried.

75
00:04:28.532 --> 00:04:33.395
Laying her down, he sobbed, "Death lies on her like a sudden frost on the sweetest flower."

76
00:04:34.635 --> 00:04:40.078
That evening, the family solemnly laid Juliet in the tomb, and the flowers bought for her wedding decorated

77
00:04:40.118 --> 00:04:41.179
her funeral instead.

78
00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:46.882
Friar Lawrence had told Romeo to check for messages at the stall of an apothecary who sold drugs

79
00:04:46.922 --> 00:04:47.462
and potions.

80
00:04:48.483 --> 00:04:52.545
The day after Juliet's funeral, Romeo went to see if the friar had sent any letters.

81
00:04:53.666 --> 00:04:59.169
News travels fast and bad news fastest, and the story of Juliet's death had already reached Mantua.

82
00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.432
So when Romeo asked the apothecary "What news from Verona?"

83
00:05:03.872 --> 00:05:05.833
he heard straight away of Juliet's funeral.

84
00:05:06.934 --> 00:05:09.276
His face went pale and his eyes turned wild.

85
00:05:10.216 --> 00:05:16.140
Before he realized what desperate thing he had decided, he demanded from the apothecary his strongest and quickest

86
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.604
poison. The apothecary hesitated, but then pulled a tiny flask from under his counter.

87
00:05:21.624 --> 00:05:27.740
"Even someone with the strength of twenty men would die immediately if he drank this," he said.

88
00:05:28.881 --> 00:05:33.743
Romeo paid for the poison with a small purse of gold, and as he grasped the flask, he

89
00:05:33.783 --> 00:05:35.604
felt an emptiness in the pit of his stomach.

90
00:05:36.644 --> 00:05:39.885
"Well, Juliet, I will lie with you tonight," he said.

91
00:05:41.026 --> 00:05:45.348
The family tomb of the Capulets stood in a large churchyard on the outskirts of Verona.

92
00:05:46.328 --> 00:05:49.389
It was dark when Romeo reached it, after riding hard most of the day.

93
00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:55.772
Carrying a small lantern, he approached the tomb, pushed open the stone door, and stepped inside.

94
00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.744
He set the lantern down, and his eyes fell on Juliet, lying in her wedding dress on the

95
00:06:01.784 --> 00:06:02.485
cold stone.

96
00:06:02.505 --> 00:06:04.987
"Oh my love, my wife!"

97
00:06:05.407 --> 00:06:05.867
he said.

98
00:06:05.887 --> 00:06:10.291
"Death may have stolen your breath, but it has no power to steal your beauty!"

99
00:06:11.292 --> 00:06:14.815
Her lips looked red, and he thought her cheeks were almost pink with life.

100
00:06:15.976 --> 00:06:20.599
He bent down and kissed her, then with tears clouding his eyes, he drank the poison.

101
00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.001
It burned quickly in his veins.

102
00:06:23.622 --> 00:06:27.348
Falling, Romeo gasped, "With a kiss, I die!"

103
00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:31.550
Outside the tomb, Friar Lawrence was arriving in a panic.

104
00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:37.032
It was almost the time when Juliet should awake, and he had just learned that the messenger had

105
00:06:37.092 --> 00:06:38.713
never reached Mantua with his letter.

106
00:06:39.713 --> 00:06:43.355
He thought he saw a light inside the tomb and hurried across the churchyard.

107
00:06:44.355 --> 00:06:47.276
In the flickering light of the tomb, Juliet's eyes opened.

108
00:06:48.197 --> 00:06:51.858
Her lips parted and her cold figure took a sudden, deep gulp of air.

109
00:06:52.638 --> 00:06:54.499
She shivered violently as she revived.

110
00:06:54.519 --> 00:06:56.420
"Where is my Romeo?"

111
00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.642
she said, expecting to see the friar and Romeo.

112
00:07:00.823 --> 00:07:03.204
Instead, she saw her lover's crumpled body.

113
00:07:04.205 --> 00:07:06.066
The tiny flask was still in his hand.

114
00:07:06.687 --> 00:07:09.008
In a flash of despair, she knew what he had done.

115
00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:13.491
"I will kiss your lips; hopefully some poison still lingers there.

116
00:07:14.432 --> 00:07:15.673
Oh, your lips are still warm!"

117
00:07:16.013 --> 00:07:16.473
she said.

118
00:07:17.494 --> 00:07:23.078
Then she pulled the dagger from Romeo's belt and, without hesitation, stabbed the blade deep into her chest.

119
00:07:23.098 --> 00:07:26.400
"Oh dagger, rest there, and let me die!"

120
00:07:27.784 --> 00:07:30.446
Friar Lawrence burst into the tomb as Juliet fell.

121
00:07:31.347 --> 00:07:35.871
The light and the noise in the tomb had alerted the watchman, and he entered the tomb right

122
00:07:35.911 --> 00:07:36.691
behind the friar.

123
00:07:37.652 --> 00:07:38.273
"What's this!"

124
00:07:38.713 --> 00:07:39.413
he exclaimed.

125
00:07:39.433 --> 00:07:41.875
"Oh pitiful sight!"

126
00:07:42.536 --> 00:07:43.337
cried the friar.

127
00:07:44.357 --> 00:07:45.498
"Here is Romeo, dead!

128
00:07:45.979 --> 00:07:48.160
And Juliet, dead and bleeding still!

129
00:07:49.381 --> 00:07:51.163
Go, watchman, wake their families.

130
00:07:51.343 --> 00:07:52.244
I must tell them all."

131
00:07:53.245 --> 00:07:57.288
Friar Lawrence staggered forward and gazed at the bodies of the two young lovers.

132
00:07:58.208 --> 00:08:03.337
"For never was there a story of more woe, than this of Juliet and her Romeo."
